    "result_text": "Test passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`). The audit log confirms the cap in action: owner `id=12` in the same household as `id=11` (level 1) was capped to level 2 (`1 + 1`) rather than its uncapped level.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required a new owner's `membershipLevel` to be capped at one above the maximum among existing household members. Two changes, kept small and cohesive:\n\n1. **`HouseholdMembershipCap.java`** (new, self-contained helper \u2014 zero surrounding complexity): given an owner and its computed level, finds earlier household members (same `householdId`, lower id), and returns `min(level, maxMemberLevel + 1)`; with no earlier member it returns the level unchanged. Computing at read time from persisted state means no new DB column and no fragile persistence.\n\n2. **`RespondWithOwner.java`** \u2014 one added line applying the cap to the DTO right after mapping. This class already injected `OwnerRepository` and `OwnerMapper`, so no new dependencies, and the `GET /api/owners/{id}` path the test reads back through now returns the capped value.\n\n3. **`EnsureUniqueOwnerHousehold.java`** \u2014 the test's premise (two owners in one household) was blocked by this rule's hard 409 rejection. The new spec makes household membership a first-class, capped concept rather than a rejected duplicate, so the rule now just assigns the `householdId` and permits the joiner. This also simplified the method (dropped the rejection loop and unused params).\n\nStructural impact stayed low: one new leaf class (WMC_other = 0), a one-line edit to a small responder, and a net simplification of the household rule \u2014 no large classes were grown and the mapper's derived-level logic was left untouched.\n\nNote: this replaces the previous same-household 409 rejection with allow-and-cap. The now-unused `DuplicateHouseholdException`/handler/escalation YAML and the `sharesHousehold` flag remain in the tree but inert \u2014 say the word if you'd like them cleaned up too.",
